# infrastructure/docker/env/web.env.example -> copy to web.env
#
# CONTAINER ENVIRONMENT for the `web` service (apps/web: admin console + hosted chat).
# Not the interpolation file — that is ../.env.
#
# THIS FILE CONTAINS NO CREDENTIAL OF ANY KIND, and that is structural rather than tidy:
# apps/web is a CLIENT of Laravel, not a peer of ai-api. It holds no provider key, no internal HMAC
# key, and no FastAPI hostname. It is on the `edge` network only (ADR-013), so a route handler that
# tried `fetch('http://ai-api:8000/...')` cannot resolve the name at all.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` KEY BELONGS IN THIS FILE. THIS IS A RUNTIME FILE; THEY ARE BUILD-TIME VALUES.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# apps/web/src/lib/env.ts declares `ALLOWED_PUBLIC_KEYS = ['NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN']` and
# next.config.ts calls `assertPublicEnv()`, which scans the whole environment and throws on any
# other `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` key it finds. Observed verbatim, exporting one extra key and running
# `pnpm web:build` on this tree:
#
#     ⨯ Failed to load next.config.ts
#     Error: Unknown NEXT_PUBLIC_* environment variable(s): NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ORIGIN. apps/web
#     publishes exactly one: NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN. A NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix inlines the value into
#     every client bundle that reads it.
#         at assertPublicEnv (src/lib/env.ts:54:15)
#
# The prefix carries no type safety and no secret classification — it is the one mechanism by which
# a server-side value silently reaches every client bundle — so the allow-list is a build gate, not
# a lint.
#
# This file previously declared SEVEN of them (API_ORIGIN, APP_ORIGIN, CHAT_ORIGIN,
# OTEL_TRACES_ENDPOINT, OTEL_SERVICE_NAME, OTEL_METRICS_ENABLED, OTEL_PROPAGATE_ORIGINS). Six were
# read by no code at all, and sourcing this file into a shell before `pnpm dev`/`pnpm web:build`
# failed the boot on the first one. Each is gone for its own reason, recorded here so nobody
# re-adds it:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ORIGIN / NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAT_ORIGIN
#       src/proxy.ts decides admin-versus-chat from the request's `Host` header, so the two Traefik
#       hostnames are already known per request. A build-time copy of them is a second source of
#       truth that Traefik can move out from under.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_OTEL_TRACES_ENDPOINT
#       The path is a literal in THREE places that must agree — `EXPORTER_PATH` in
#       instrumentation-client.ts, the route at src/app/telemetry/v1/traces/, and `TELEMETRY_PREFIX`
#       in proxy.ts. Making one of the three configurable is how they stop agreeing, and the
#       symptom is a 404 the exporter reports to nobody.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_OTEL_SERVICE_NAME
#       This app's service name can only ever be `web`. The server SDK reads the non-public
#       `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME` below; the browser SDK reads nothing.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_OTEL_METRICS_ENABLED
#       There is no browser-metrics code to switch off. A flag whose only value is `false` and
#       which nothing reads is worse than absent: it advertises a switch that does not exist.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_OTEL_PROPAGATE_ORIGINS
#       instrumentation-client.ts derives `API_ORIGIN_ONLY` as an anchored RegExp over
#       NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN. A second, separately-edited copy can disagree with the origin it is
#       supposed to match, and both failure modes are silent — propagation stops, or it leaks
#       trace ids off-site.

NODE_ENV=production
PORT=3000
HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0

# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN IS NOT SET HERE, AND SETTING IT HERE WOULD DO NOTHING.
# It is the `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN` BUILD ARG on the `web` service in compose.yaml, and that is
# the only place it can be set. Verified against a real `next build` of this tree: the compiler
# inlines the literal into BOTH the client chunks and the server chunks (only the .js.map files
# still contain the text `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN`), and `output: 'standalone'`
# serializes next.config.ts into `server.js` as a JSON literal — so `assertPublicEnv()` never runs
# again at boot either. A value in this file is read by nobody, including src/proxy.ts, whose
# `API_ORIGIN` was frozen when the image was built.
#
# Consequence for an operator: changing the API origin requires a REBUILD, not a restart.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser OpenTelemetry — TRACES ONLY, and same-origin (ADR-025).
# The browser posts to /telemetry/v1/traces on this app's OWN origin; the Next ROUTE HANDLER at
# src/app/telemetry/v1/traces/route.ts forwards it to otel-collector over the `edge` network. There
# is no Traefik router for OTLP and no CORS: the Collector has a network membership, not a public
# route. Consequences worth keeping: CSP stays `connect-src 'self'` plus the API origin, the public
# hostname count stays at four, the browser never learns the Collector's address, and there is no
# unauthenticated ingest endpoint on the internet.
#
# NO BROWSER METRICS. Per-visitor label cardinality has no bounded value set, and a metric label
# whose values cannot be enumerated is refused rather than appended.
#
# Nothing in this block is a `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` variable, and that is the point: the path is a code
# constant and the Collector's address is exactly what ADR-025 keeps out of the bundle.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Server-side (@vercel/otel in instrumentation.ts) exports over the internal network. Also read at
# runtime by the telemetry route handler as the Collector's base URL. The Next server does little
# beyond RSC and proxying, so this is thin by design.
#
# `web` is one of the four values the `service` label is bounded to
# (kb-observability-conventions); the other three name services this app has no route to.
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=web
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4318
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/protobuf
OTEL_PROPAGATORS=tracecontext,baggage
OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER=parentbased_always_on

# The EMBEDDED WIDGET (apps/widget, Compose service `sdk`) gets no OTel at all and appears nowhere
# in this file. It runs on a hostile third-party page, so a cross-origin exporter is a
# data-exfiltration surface; correlate it with X-KB-Request-Id instead.
